{"name":"Banded Tulip","hex":"#e0d3bd","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"banded","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Marked with bands of colour.","Divided into bands."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of band"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/banded"},{"term":"tulip","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A type of flowering plant, genus Tulipa.","The flower of this plant."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tulip"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cinctura lilium","blocks":[{"text":"Cinctura lilium, common name the banded tulip, is a species of sea snail, a marine gastropod mollusk in the family Fasciolariidae, the spindle snails, the tulip snails and their allies."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/01/Banded_Tulip_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cinctura_lilium","wikidata":"Q2387009"},{"title":"Tulip","blocks":[{"text":"Tulips are spring-blooming perennial herbaceous bulbiferous geophytes in the Tulipa genus. Their flowers are usually large, showy, and brightly coloured, generally red, orange, pink, yellow, or white. They often have a different coloured blotch at the base of the tepals, internally. Because of a degree of variability within the populations and a long history of cultivation, classification has been complex and controversial. The tulip is a member of the lily family, Liliaceae, along with 14 other genera, where it is most closely related to Amana, Erythronium, and Gagea in the tribe Lilieae."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/%D7%A6%D7%91%D7%A2%D7%95%D7%A0%D7%99%D7%9D.JPG/330px-%D7%A6%D7%91%D7%A2%D7%95%D7%A0%D7%99%D7%9D.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tulip","wikidata":"Q93201"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wi